  "content": {
    "id": "p10-q30-response",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "30",
    "prompt": "Suppose $n\\in\\mathbb{N}$. Prove that if $2^n-1$ is prime, then $n$ is prime.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$n$ must be prime.\n\nIf $n=ab$ is composite, then $2^n-1$ factors into two integers greater than $1$, contradicting primality.",
      "worked": "If $2^n-1$ is prime, then $n>1$, since $2^1-1=1$ is not prime (and $2^0-1=0$ is not prime).\n\nSuppose $n$ is composite. Write $n=ab$, where $a,b>1$. Then\n$$\\begin{align*}2^n-1&=2^{ab}-1\\\\2^n-1&=(2^a)^b-1\\\\2^n-1&=(2^a-1)\\left(\\sum_{j=0}^{b-1}2^{aj}\\right).\\end{align*}$$\nBoth factors are integers greater than $1$: $2^a-1\\ge3$, and the sum contains $1+2^a$. Thus $2^n-1$ is composite, a contradiction. Therefore, $n$ is prime.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 60
  },
